---
// Fact-forward pass (matches the corrected helical-piers-deep-dive.astro
// treatment). No MFRC source page and no glossary entry exist for this
// one -- it's a catch-all by definition, not a discrete technique, so
// this page is deliberately the shortest of the set rather than padded
// to match the others. No misconceptions/Q&A section (dropped sitewide).
//
// Per Tim: added the real pattern behind TLS's custom work -- owner-
// designed brackets, adapted techniques, and hybrid systems combining
// two methods when the problem didn't split cleanly into one category.
// Framed deliberately as "not secret, just judgment earned over time"
// (same register as the Methods page's soil-knowledge framing) rather
// than a special-knowledge sales pitch -- Tim raised the "you can't
// replace our brains" angle but was unsure how hard to push it, and
// that's the wrong direction to push it on this site. [TIM: ...] still
// marks the door open for an actual named job story if he has one --
// that would land harder than the general pattern alone.
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const title = 'Custom Solutions in New Mexico Foundation Repair | TLS Foundations';
const summary =
  'Some sites do not fit neatly into a standard category -- historic adobe with no standard bearing reference, an addition built on a different foundation type than the original structure, a site with utility conflicts or contaminated soil that rules out standard equipment. When that happens, the repair gets built around the actual conditions instead of forcing the problem into a template.';
const description =
  'What "custom solutions" means, when a job genuinely falls outside the standard toolkit, and why that is a real category rather than a catch-all excuse.';

const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: 'Custom Solutions in New Mexico Foundation Repair',
    description,
    about: description,
    author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'TLS Foundations' },
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'TLS Foundations' },
    url: 'https://www.tlsfoundations.com/custom-solutions-deep-dive',
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://www.tlsfoundations.com' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Foundation Repair Methods', item: 'https://www.tlsfoundations.com/foundation-repair-methods' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Custom Solutions', item: 'https://www.tlsfoundations.com/custom-solutions-deep-dive' },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  footer="minimal"
  schema={schema}
  summary={summary}
  pageName="Custom Solutions"
  nextPage={{ path: '/foundation-repair-methods', name: 'Foundation Repair Methods' }}
>
  <div class="tls-article" id="top">
    <section class="page-hero">
      <div class="page-hero-inner">
        <span class="hero-eyebrow">Methods · Custom Solutions</span>
        <h1>Custom Solutions —<br /><em>When Nothing Standard Fits</em></h1>
        <p class="page-hero-sub">Some sites don't fit neatly into a category. What actually pushes a job into "custom" territory, and why that's a real answer, not a vague one.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="article-outer">
      <article class="article-body">
        <p class="article-lede">This is the one entry in the toolkit that isn't a technique at all — it's an admission that the other seven don't cover everything, and a commitment to build around the actual site rather than force it into whichever tool happens to be on the truck.</p>

        <div class="article-section" id="what-it-is">
          <h2>Where the Standard <em>Toolkit Runs Out</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">When a site doesn't fit any of the standard methods cleanly, the repair gets built around the actual conditions instead of the problem getting forced into a template that almost works.</p>
          <p>That's a meaningfully different claim than "we can do anything" — it's specifically about what happens when the standard toolkit runs out. Historic adobe construction with no standard bearing reference to design against. An addition built on a different foundation type than the original structure, where the two don't move the same way. A site with utility conflicts or contaminated soil that rules out the equipment a standard method would need. The common thread across all of these is that none of the other seven methods were built with that specific problem in mind.</p>
          <p>Two things worth being direct about: "custom" doesn't mean automatically more expensive — it means the standard approach doesn't apply, and sometimes a custom approach is actually simpler than forcing a standard method to work. And a genuinely custom solution isn't a sign of uncertainty; it requires understanding the site well enough to know that none of the standard options actually fit, which is more investigation, not less.</p>
          <p>Off-the-shelf products and standard methods are good for exactly what they're built for, and most jobs don't need anything else. Custom work is different only because it can't be bought pre-made: a bracket built for a connection nothing in a catalog was designed to handle, a standard technique adapted to a site it wasn't written for, a hybrid system built by combining two methods because the problem didn't split cleanly into one category. That kind of solution comes from having solved something close to it before — not secret, just judgment earned over time.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="article-section" id="examples">
          <h2>Real Jobs That <em>Didn't Fit the Standard List</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">In practice, custom work at TLS has meant designing brackets that didn't exist off the shelf for a connection a standard part wasn't built to handle, adapting a standard installation technique to a site condition it wasn't originally designed for, and building hybrid systems that combine two methods when neither one alone was the right answer.</p>
          <p>[TIM: if you have a specific job in mind — even loosely described, no address needed — that would make this section land even harder than the general pattern above.]</p>
        </div>

        <a href="#top" class="back-to-top">↑ Back to top</a>

        <p class="closing-link"><a href="/foundation-repair-methods" class="body-link">← Back to Foundation Repair Methods</a></p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .tls-article {
